#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cc47893-4576-56e2-b51b-ce99eaf337e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547490} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b5802-b097-565d-8a8d-3cbc86eb5664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547496} {:answer/id #uuid "bba43083-7060-5fde-819c-d1d64e90ed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547717} {:answer/id #uuid "cabe3545-1601-5fc2-b776-2d1f2769c466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547748} {:answer/id #uuid "fe61a9af-bb8a-5a5e-a47a-8216aa26ccd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba43083-7060-5fde-819c-d1d64e90ed78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547717} :answer-field/id #uuid "87155073-92f3-57b6-9c30-b010bce3c156" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dc89317-431f-536c-84f1-f80a52b02432" :question/math-academy-id "q-152279" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x+2$ and the line $y=2-2x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${y}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x+2 \\\\ y=2-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+2 & =2-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ x(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=0.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2-2{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2-2(-4) \\\\  & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,10).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =2-2{x}_{2} \\\\  & =2-2(0) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}=10,\\,{y}_{2}=2,$ and\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=10+2=12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/94/941d698059cfd145f263cbdde048fc4a32245cdad4bc6bb6d7a504d005b6c231.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "340c4803-d8c0-59d9-a251-82d6850f70ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547423} {:answer/id #uuid "3a39b58c-130d-5ebb-836a-1129846e881e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547440} {:answer/id #uuid "5757b43b-f6e1-569c-b37a-4f6109bc720f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547511} {:answer/id #uuid "87f5df3e-d889-51a5-9bbf-55a47147c1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547611} {:answer/id #uuid "9106e55b-9dcf-53a3-80ef-e117f396ee65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9106e55b-9dcf-53a3-80ef-e117f396ee65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547639} :answer-field/id #uuid "83300691-a442-5ef8-b2cc-b11ca96f7be8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95764a8d-23d1-5507-836a-2817991f65d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152314" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+1,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ y=x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1 & =x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+1-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=-1,$ and $c=1-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-1)\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{1-4+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{4k-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k-3 & <0 \\\\ 4k & <3 \\\\ k & <\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be915f26-a2fb-5d29-9b1c-a6e737760c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be915f26-a2fb-5d29-9b1c-a6e737760c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547721} :answer-field/id #uuid "55faa739-ebaa-5c90-a047-3bc90cd8f091" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f8e2a0-ea50-50ee-b075-78ac59d58fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f8e2a0-ea50-50ee-b075-78ac59d58fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547821} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c1656d-f914-59ef-862b-d658127e5cce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dbc8468-e68d-529c-9edc-f8593cda9fbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-250075" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x-1$ and the line $y=-2x-13$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x-1 \\\\ y=-2x-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-1 & =-2x-13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-4)-13 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,-5).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =-2{x}_{2}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-3)-13 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-3,-7).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782a5e0fbe4c362ca17e86a11f017f6909873cb1f06f0d801d6d958bd1c8ab44.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e632f5-c50c-5da4-bb1c-9c0ced37d622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e632f5-c50c-5da4-bb1c-9c0ced37d622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547363} :answer-field/id #uuid "1917052b-8eb2-50f9-8460-330446612d70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547853} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db2c4ef0-9137-54be-b391-5354ceba0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db2c4ef0-9137-54be-b391-5354ceba0e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547778} :answer-field/id #uuid "db3f8322-9a29-5bb3-823b-39d461179c3c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc9c836c-676e-5aa5-b4fe-1701bb866c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-250103" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+1$ and the line $y=3x$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+1 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+1 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}+5x+1-3x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-1.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x \\\\  & =3(-1) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only point of intersection is $(-1,-3).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a5db47f31fc3e7be1afa4c5413e193c0751b68998e573093c69da5f2441b5a7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60ee5989-15d3-5396-8ca7-f4be1dd32b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60ee5989-15d3-5396-8ca7-f4be1dd32b99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547527} :answer-field/id #uuid "70af0a0d-0f7f-5758-a36e-34036e71a3d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6296c1c8-c2d4-5228-a381-4cbde8c22806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6296c1c8-c2d4-5228-a381-4cbde8c22806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547530} :answer-field/id #uuid "88ead4fe-b75d-5d94-a601-d98ef461e331" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28de8699-3497-5296-9483-734e67042dfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-250220" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x+4$ and the line $y=5x$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x+4 \\\\ y=5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x+4 & =5x \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=2.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. Let's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x \\\\  & =5(2) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only point of intersection is $(2,10).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/268de319ae56302e9b633e09a4c74a0ebd62f3d8ed47829f709036082a644bf4.png)"}]}
